        !!! About Dr's comment for registration detect id tu, ni explanation untuk code skrg:
        
        Your lecturer is saying: "Don't just look at a 5-digit ID to guess if someone is an invigilator. The database must explicitly tell the app their role."

        Right now, your registration form detects the role based on the ID format (A123 vs 12345) and then saves role: 'student' or role: 'invigilator' into the users collection.

        Because of this, your AuthWrapper already works exactly how your lecturer wants! When you log in, the app ignores the ID and checks Firestore:

        dart
        // In auth_wrapper.dart
        final role = roleSnapshot.data; 
        if (role == 'student') return const StudentDashboard();
        if (role == 'invigilator') return const InvigilatorDashboardView();
        Why you should NOT divide the collection:
        If you create separate students and lecturers collections, your entire app will break.

        AuthWrapper: You would have to write complex code to check two collections to find out who logged in.

        Registration: You would have to write if (role == 'student') { save to students } else { save to lecturers }.

        Queries: Your manual check-in function would have to search the students collection, but your login function searches users. It creates a massive mess.

        The Industry Standard: You keep everyone in one users collection, and you use the role field to differentiate them. This is exactly what Google, Facebook, and Amazon do.